<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tasciovanus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tasciovanus was a king of the Catuvellauni in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the late 1st century BCE, known from his abundant coinage. Many of his coins were minted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verlamion</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-albans">St Albans</a>, and some bear the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> title RICON or RIGON, meaning king. He is generally thought to have been the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tasciovanus.jpg" alt="Tasciovanus" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numisantica · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celtic_gold_stater_Catuvellauni_Tasciovanus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tasciovanus, also found as Tasciovantis, is known only from coins. He appears to have become king of the Catuvellauni around 20 BCE, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verlamion</a>, and he is believed to have moved the tribal capital there from an earlier settlement near present-day Wheathampstead. Recent excavations on the southern plateau of the Ver valley found mid-first-century Roman pottery and Roman republican denarii minted between 100 and 80 BCE, evidence of a settlement there from the early 1st century BCE.</p><p>For a short time, around 15 to 10 BCE, he issued coins from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a>, apparently displacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addedomarus">Addedomarus</a> of the Trinovantes. He then struck coins again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verlamion</a>. The legend RICON stands for Rigonos, a Common Brittonic word for a great, divine or legitimate king. Other abbreviated names on his coins, such as DIAS, SEGO and ANDOCO, are usually read as the names of co-rulers or subordinate kings, though they may be mint-marks. He was succeeded by Cunobeline, who ruled mainly from Camulodunum, while Epaticcus, another son, extended his power westward into the lands of the Atrebates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>A genealogy in the medieval Welsh manuscript British Library Harley MS 3859 has the line Caratauc map Cinbelin map Teuhant, that is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a>, son of Tasciovanus. The three are in the right order though in the wrong historical setting, and the change in their names suggests a long oral tradition. Teuhant would be Techwant or Teichwant in modern Welsh.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a>'s fictional Historia Regum Britanniae of 1136 turned him into Tenvantius, son of Lud. In this story he and his brother Androgeus were minors when their father died, so the throne passed to their uncle Cassibelanus. Tenvantius became Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and succeeded his uncle, and his son Kimbelinus had been raised at the court of Augustus. Middle Welsh versions name him Teneufan and Trahayant, and as Tenewan ap Lludd he is claimed as an ancestor of the Mathrafal dynasty in the Mostyn manuscript 117.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tasciovanus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the late 1st century BCE their king <b>Tasciovanus</b> was minting coins at Verlamion, near modern St Albans.</blockquote><a href="/state/catuvellauni">Catuvellauni</a></li></ul></section>
